.TH HSETROOT 1 "2026" "hsetroot" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hsetroot \- yet another wallpaper application
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hsetroot
[\fB\-\-command1\fR [\fIarg\fR...]] [\fB\-\-command2\fR [\fIarg\fR...]]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
hsetroot is a tool which allows you to compose wallpapers ("root pixmaps")
for X. It has a lot of options like rendering gradients, solids, images but
it also allows you to perform manipulations on those things, or chain them
together.
.PP
It sets the result as the root window background, including the
\fB_XROOTPMAP_ID\fR and \fBESETROOT_PMAP_ID\fR properties used by compositors
and pseudo-transparent terminals.
.PP
Commands are applied in the order given, each one on top of what's drawn so far.
Manipulations (\fB\-\-fliph\fR, \fB\-\-blur\fR, ...) affect what's already drawn,
settings (\fB\-\-alpha\fR, \fB\-\-fg\fR, \fB\-\-add\fR, ...) affect what comes after.
A setting that nothing after it uses only prints a warning.
Without any commands, the background is set to black.
.PP
Every command can be written as \fB\-\-name\fR \fIvalue\fR, \fB\-\-name\fR=\fIvalue\fR
or the old \fB\-name\fR \fIvalue\fR.
.PP
Images and gradients are drawn on each monitor (as listed by
\fBxrandr \-\-listmonitors\fR, or Xinerama heads on servers without RandR 1.5).
\fB\-\-root\fR treats all of them as one big screen, \fB\-\-output\fR limits the
following commands to one monitor.
.PP
hsetroot draws on the X screen from \fB$DISPLAY\fR (\fB:0.1\fR is screen 1).
Most setups have just one X screen, with any number of monitors.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Generic options"
.TP
\fB\-\-root\fR
Treat multiple displays as one big screen (ignore xrandr outputs).
.TP
\fB\-\-screens\fR \fIint\fR
Draw on several X screens, as a bitmask (1 = screen 0, 2 = screen 1, 3 = both;
0 is an error).
.TP
\fB\-\-output\fR \fIname\fR
Draw only on this output from now on, \fBall\fR for every output again.
Names are the ones from \fBxrandr \-\-listmonitors\fR or \fBhsr\-outputs\fR.
Can't be used with \fB\-\-root\fR, except for \fBall\fR.
Settings such as \fB\-\-alpha\fR and colors added with \fB\-\-add\fR carry
over to the next output; use \fB\-\-clear\fR for a new gradient.
.SS "Gradients"
.TP
\fB\-\-add\fR \fIcolor\fR
Add color to range using distance 1.
.TP
\fB\-\-addd\fR \fIcolor\fR \fIdistance\fR
Add color to range using custom distance.
.TP
\fB\-\-gradient\fR \fIangle\fR
Render gradient using specified angle, on each monitor.
.TP
\fB\-\-clear\fR
Clear the color range.
.SS "Solid"
.TP
\fB\-\-solid\fR \fIcolor\fR
Render a solid using the specified color.
.SS "Image files"
.TP
\fB\-\-center\fR \fIimage\fR
Render an image centered on screen.
.TP
\fB\-\-cover\fR \fIimage\fR
Render an image centered on screen scaled so that all of screen is covered.
.TP
\fB\-\-tile\fR \fIimage\fR
Render an image tiled.
.TP
\fB\-\-full\fR \fIimage\fR
Render an image maximum aspect.
.TP
\fB\-\-extend\fR \fIimage\fR
Render an image maximum aspect, and copy 1px border taken from the image over
the remaining area so that no black borders are shown.
.TP
\fB\-\-fill\fR \fIimage\fR
Render an image stretched.
.SS "Bitmaps"
As in \fBxsetroot\fR(1), tiled from the top left corner. Unlike xsetroot, the
colors have to come before the bitmap.
.TP
\fB\-\-fg\fR \fIcolor\fR
Foreground color for the following bitmaps (default black).
.TP
\fB\-\-bg\fR \fIcolor\fR
Background color for the following bitmaps (default white).
.TP
\fB\-\-rv\fR, \fB\-\-reverse\fR
Swap the foreground and background colors.
.TP
\fB\-\-bitmap\fR \fIxbm\fR
Render an X bitmap file tiled.
.TP
\fB\-\-mod\fR \fIx\fR \fIy\fR
Render a tiled grid with vertical lines \fIx\fR pixels apart and horizontal
lines \fIy\fR pixels apart. The pattern repeats every 16 pixels; 0 fills
that direction, and values above 16 give one line per tile.
.TP
\fB\-\-gray\fR, \fB\-\-grey\fR
Render a gray (checkerboard) pattern.
.SS "Manipulations"
.TP
\fB\-\-tint\fR \fIcolor\fR
Tint the current image.
.TP
\fB\-\-blur\fR \fIradius\fR
Blur the current image.
.TP
\fB\-\-sharpen\fR \fIradius\fR
Sharpen the current image.
.TP
\fB\-\-contrast\fR \fIamount\fR
Adjust contrast of current image.
.TP
\fB\-\-brightness\fR \fIamount\fR
Adjust brightness of current image.
.TP
\fB\-\-gamma\fR \fIamount\fR
Adjust gamma level of current image.
.TP
\fB\-\-flipv\fR
Flip the current image vertically.
.TP
\fB\-\-fliph\fR
Flip the current image horizontally.
.TP
\fB\-\-flipd\fR
Flip the current image diagonally.
.SS "Misc"
.TP
\fB\-\-alpha\fR \fI0\-255\fR
Adjust alpha level for colors and images.
.TP
\fB\-\-write\fR \fIfilename\fR
Write current image to file.
.TP
\fB\-\-verbose\fR
Print which outputs images are drawn on.
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR, \fB\-h\fR
Show the help.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Show the version.
.SH COLORS
Colors are in the \fB#rgb\fR, \fB#rrggbb\fR, \fB#rrggbbaa\fR, \fBrgb:r/g/b\fR
formats or an X color name (see \fBXParseColor\fR(3)).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on any error (unknown option, bad value, image or color, unknown
output, no active monitors), in which case the background is left alone.
123 when the X display can't be opened.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
hsetroot \-\-cover wallpaper.jpg
One image, scaled to cover each monitor.
.TP
hsetroot \-\-root \-\-fill panorama.jpg
One image stretched over all monitors.
.TP
hsetroot \-\-output eDP\-1 \-\-cover laptop.jpg \-\-output HDMI\-1 \-\-cover tv.jpg
A different image on each monitor.
.TP
hsetroot \-\-add '#1e1e2e' \-\-add '#89b4fa' \-\-gradient 30
A gradient on each monitor.
.TP
hsetroot \-\-fg gray20 \-\-bg black \-\-mod 16 16
The xsetroot grid.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBhsr\-outputs\fR (lists the monitors hsetroot sees, \fB\-\-csv\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR),
\fBxsetroot\fR(1), \fBxrandr\fR(1)
